    <!-- 1. Motivation & Research -->
    <section id="research" class="section">
        <div class="section-header">
            <span class="section-number">01</span>
            <span class="section-tag">Research</span>
            <h2 class="section-title">Motivation & Research</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="section-content">
            <h3>The Why (200-word statement)</h3>
            <p>
                We chose the EAP student support track because our initial research revealed a critical pain point: most students experience high anxiety and "brain blanks" during English discussions, which prevents them from practicing before class and building confidence. Existing tools fail to address both the psychological safety and real-time feedback needs of multilingual learners. XJTAIK was created to bridge this gap by providing a low-pressure, AI-powered practice space that simulates real discussions while offering personalized, non-judgmental support. Our goal is to help students build confidence step-by-step in a safe environment.
            </p>

            <h3>The Gap (4 Papers + 4 Commercial Products)</h3>
            <p><strong>Academic Papers - Strengths:</strong></p>
            <ul>
                <li>Validated the link between language anxiety and unwillingness to communicate in multilingual contexts</li>
                <li>Proven that low-stakes practice environments reduce anxiety and build confidence</li>
                <li>Outlined frameworks for AI-driven scaffolding in collaborative learning</li>
            </ul>
            <p><strong>Academic Papers - Missed Opportunities:</strong></p>
            <ul>
                <li>Few studies address real-time, non-intrusive support during live discussions</li>
                <li>Lack of focus on granular, turn-by-turn feedback for EAP contexts</li>
                <li>No frameworks combining psychological safety with data-driven progress tracking</li>
            </ul>

            <p style="margin-top:1.5rem;"><strong>Commercial Products - Strengths:</strong></p>
            <ul>
                <li>Doubao: Strong AI conversation and summarization features</li>
                <li>Padlet: Supports collaborative discussion spaces</li>
                <li>ArguText: Built for structured argumentation practice</li>
                <li>Zoom/Teams: Common tools for virtual discussions</li>
            </ul>
            <p><strong>Commercial Products - Limitations:</strong></p>
            <ul>
                <li>None offer EAP-specific scaffolding or anxiety-reducing design</li>
                <li>No multi-user simulation with role-taking and turn management</li>
                <li>Lack of turn-by-turn participation tracking and personalized feedback reports</li>
            </ul>

            <h3>The Stakeholders & Personas</h3>
            <p><strong>Primary Users: EAP Students</strong></p>
            <p>Persona 1: An anxious Year 1 student who avoids speaking in class, struggles with thinking of ideas mid-discussion, and wants to practice before group work.</p>
            <p style="margin-top:1rem;"><strong>Secondary Users: EAP Teachers</strong></p>
            <p>Persona 2: An EAP lecturer who cannot monitor every group simultaneously and needs data-driven insights to identify students who need extra support.</p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 2. User Requirements -->
    <section id="requirements" class="section">
        <div class="section-header">
            <span class="section-number">02</span>
            <span class="section-tag">Needs</span>
            <h2 class="section-title">User Requirements</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="section-content">
            <h3>User Journey Map</h3>
            <p>
                The journey starts with "Setup Anxiety" (fear of joining discussions), moves to "Practice" (blanking out, running out of ideas), then "Feedback" (uncertainty about performance), and ends with "Success" (building confidence). Key pain points: silence anxiety, idea gaps, fear of judgment, and lack of actionable feedback.
            </p>

            <h3>3 Must-Have "Playful" Requirements</h3>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Low-pressure, judgment-free practice:</strong> Anonymous rooms and AI partners to reduce anxiety</li>
                <li><strong>Real-time, unobtrusive hints:</strong> Non-forced prompts to help users keep the conversation going</li>
                <li><strong>Engaging feedback:</strong> Summaries and progress tracking that feels supportive, not punitive</li>
            </ul>

            <h3>Evidence of Life</h3>
            <p>
                Our team conducted 5 user interviews and 2 site observations (including EAP classroom sessions) to validate pain points. We have photos/videos of users sharing their anxiety experiences, practicing discussions, and testing early paper prototypes.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 3. Ideation & Alternatives -->
    <section id="ideation" class="section">
        <div class="section-header">
            <span class="section-number">03</span>
            <span class="section-tag">Ideation</span>
            <h2 class="section-title">Ideation & Alternatives</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="section-content">
            <h3>Crazy Eights Sketches</h3>
            <p>
                We created 8 rapid hand-drawn sketches exploring different UI concepts: lobby rooms, discussion controls, hint triggers, feedback dashboards, anonymous profiles, role assignment, and summary screens. These sketches helped us quickly test ideas like manual vs. automatic hints, turn-taking controls, and different feedback visualizations.
            </p>

            <h3>Design Alternatives Comparison</h3>
            <p><strong>Option 1: Automatic, constant AI hints</strong></p>
            <p>Pros: Never runs out of ideas. Cons: Too intrusive, removes agency, feels like the AI is speaking for the user. <br>We rejected this to preserve user ownership of the conversation.</p>

            <p style="margin-top:1rem;"><strong>Option 2: No AI hints, only post-discussion feedback</strong></p>
            <p>Pros: No disruption to flow. Cons: Leaves users stuck in the moment, doesn’t address "brain blank" anxiety. <br>We rejected this because it failed to solve the core real-time pain point.</p>

            <p style="margin-top:1rem;"><strong>Option 3 (Chosen): User-controlled, context-aware hints</strong></p>
            <p>Balances support and agency: hints only appear on demand or during silence, with manual turn-taking. This keeps the conversation natural while still providing a safety net.</p>

